(1-\\theta )^{2-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(2n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{4}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{5}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{6}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(2n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=1+2+2+1+2+2=10.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+10\\opera