078 17592186576411 true] [17592186645745 1071 "Let $f(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Find ${g}^{′}(1)$ given the function is defined by $g(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186645747 1071 "Write the following series in sigma notation in terms of index $n.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 32+16+8+…={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\n$${{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186645747 1071 "Write the following series in sigma notation in terms of index $n.$\n\n$32+16+8+…={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }${{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186645753 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x|>7 \\\\ 3|x|>7 \\\\ |x|>\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{3})\\cup (\\frac{7}{3},\\infty ).\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|3n|>7$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\{…,-4,-3\\}\\cup \\{3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|3q|>7$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186645753 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x|>7 \\\\ 3|x|>7 \\\\ |x|>\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{3})\\cup (\\frac{7}{3},\\infty ).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|3n|>7$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\{…,-4,-3\\}\\cup \\{3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|3q|>7$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186645762 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{8}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}\n$$ and $x=4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of $-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186645762 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{8}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of $-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186646271 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{3}{8}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}(4-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}(2-t)(2+t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-2,0,2.$\n\nSince $t>0$, we discard solutions $t=-2,0.$\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186646271 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{3}{8}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}(4-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}(2-t)(2+t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-2,0,2.$\n\nSince $t>0$, we discard solutions $t=-2,0.$\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186646280 1071 "Find the sum $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1-e}{e}-\\frac{(1-e)^{2}}{2{e}^{2}}+\\frac{(1-e)^{3}}{3{e}^{3}}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(1-e)^{n}}{n{e}^{n}}+⋯.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186646280 1071 "Find the sum $\\frac{1-e}{e}-\\frac{(1-e)^{2}}{2{e}^{2}}+\\frac{(1-e)^{3}}{3{e}^{3}}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(1-e)^{n}}{n{e}^{n}}+⋯.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186646294 1071 "Find ${M}^{T}$ given that \n    $$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186646294 1071 "Find ${M}^{T}$ given that \n    $M=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false]